# 6 Evaluation

<!-- Generated by tools/hls-est-validate/thesis/make_thesis_tables.py from templates/06-evaluation.md.in on {{generated}}.
     Do not edit: every number below is a placeholder filled from the result files named in hls-doc/thesis/README.md.
     Final evaluation run: {{newest_run}} (hls-est {{newest_hlsest_git}}, cgeist md5 {{newest_cgeist_md5}} = Polygeist {{newest_polygeist}}). -->

This chapter measures how closely hls-est reproduces the BRAM decisions of Vitis HLS 2025.1 [@amd2025ug1399] on the HLSFactory catalogue [@abikaram2024hlsfactory]. It covers seven questions: how accurate the final version is, by design total and per array (6.2); what a naive model scores on the same designs (6.2); how the accuracy developed over the rounds of the method (6.3); what the errors look like (6.4); which modelled rules contribute how much (6.5); how fast hls-est is compared with a Vitis run (6.6); and which designs are still out of band, and why (6.7). Section 6.8 lists the threats to validity and what the evaluation does not cover.

**In-sample evaluation.** The catalogue evaluated here is the same catalogue on which the rules were found: every round started from its mismatches (Chapter 3). All accuracy numbers in this chapter are therefore in-sample numbers, not an estimate of accuracy on unseen designs (Section 6.8).

## 6.1 Set-up

**Ground truth.** Every Vitis number is the `csynth` BRAM_18K total of a Vitis HLS 2025.1 run. All runs use the part `xc7z020clg400-1`, a 10 ns clock, `flow_target=vivado` and `syn.array_partition.throughput_driven=off`, and are stored in `results/vitis.json`. The parser reads the `* Memory:` utilisation table and `csynth.xml`, never the storage report (see the harness guide).

**Corpus.** The corpus is the HLSFactory_v2 catalogue: {{all_designs}} designs in {{datasets_n}} datasets. Two designs are counted as copies only when they have the same cgeist MLIR byte for byte, the same top function and the same Vitis total. By that test, {{duplicate_copies}} of the compared designs are copies of another compared design: the `WIP` dataset repeats `stream_hls` designs, and `polybench__reproducible` repeats some of its own. The tables therefore also report a *distinct sources* view.

**Pipeline.** Every design goes through cgeist with the real Vitis headers (`--shim real`), then through the default hls-est pipeline, under a limit of 120 s per design. Each run is one result file of `run_hlsest.py`.

**Metrics.** A design is *compared* when both tools return a BRAM total and the hls-est record was produced from the source Vitis synthesised. It is *exact* when the two totals are equal. It is *in band* when it is exact or over-counts by at most {{band_over_pct}} % of the Vitis value; under-counts are always out of band (the success criterion, Section 1.5). The other metrics are:
- Σ|err|: the sum of |hls-est − Vitis| over compared designs.
- *Weighted error*: Σ|err| divided by the summed Vitis BRAM.
- *Relative error*: (hls-est − Vitis) / Vitis, defined only for designs where Vitis reports at least one BRAM.

**Re-scoring.** Every run in this chapter is re-scored against the current `results/vitis.json` by `thesis_data.score`, which applies the same rules as `compare.py`. For the newest run, the generator checks that the re-scoring reproduces the run's own `compare.py` summary; the check reports: {{rescore_check}}. Older runs can differ from the numbers recorded at the time by a design or two (Table 6.5, column "as recorded"). The cause is ground-truth entries merged or re-keyed since those runs.

## 6.2 Headline accuracy

{{table:headline}}

*Table 6.1. Headline accuracy by design total. "Distinct sources" counts each group of byte-identical copies once; "fits the device" keeps the designs for which Vitis allocates at most the {{device_capacity}} BRAM_18K of the part (`tables/ch06-headline.md`, `.tex`).*

The {{newest_label}} covers {{all_designs}} designs:
- cgeist compiles {{all_cgeist_ok}} of them, and hls-est returns an estimate for {{all_hlsest_ok}}. cgeist fails on {{all_cgeist_fail}} designs; `done-exclusions.md` matches them to the Vitis rejections of the same sources. Of the compiled designs, {{all_hlsest_fail}} end without an estimate: in {{all_top_not_found}} hls-est finds no function with the top's name, and {{all_hlsest_timeout}} reach the 120 s limit. Only {{all_hlsest_fail_with_vitis}} of these {{all_hlsest_fail}} have a Vitis result.
- Vitis has no result for {{all_estimated_no_vitis}} of the {{all_hlsest_ok}} designs hls-est estimates ({{all_no_vitis}} of all {{all_designs}} designs): Vitis rejected them, failed or timed out.
- {{all_compared}} designs are compared. Of these, {{all_exact}} ({{all_exact_pct}} %) are exact and {{all_in_band}} ({{all_in_band_pct}} %) are in band.
- Of the {{all_out_of_band}} designs out of band, {{all_under}} are under-counts and {{all_over_gt15}} over-count by more than {{band_over_pct}} %.
- Over all compared designs, Σ|err| = {{all_abs_err}} BRAM_18K against {{all_vitis_bram_total}} BRAM_18K that Vitis allocates, a weighted error of {{all_weighted_err_pct}} %. The mean |relative error| is {{all_mean_abs_rel_pct}} %. Among the designs that are not exact, the median |relative error| is {{all_median_abs_rel_nonexact_pct}} %.

Five things qualify these numbers; the strictest view (point 3) combines the first two.
1. **Zero-BRAM designs.** For {{all_compared_vitis_zero}} compared designs, Vitis reports zero BRAM, and hls-est matches {{all_compared_vitis_zero_exact}} of them. These designs are not trivial for the model: a zero means that every array is a register, a LUTRAM (N5) or was removed (N1, N1a, N66). Still, they lift the exact rate. Over the {{nonzero_compared}} designs where Vitis reports at least one BRAM, {{nonzero_exact}} are exact and {{nonzero_in_band}} are in band.
2. **Copies.** Counting each distinct source once, {{distinct_exact}} of {{distinct_compared}} designs are exact and {{distinct_in_band}} are in band. The weighted error is {{distinct_weighted_err_pct}} %.
3. **The strictest view: distinct sources with at least one BRAM.** Without copies and without zero-BRAM designs, {{distinct_nonzero_exact}} of {{distinct_nonzero_compared}} designs ({{distinct_nonzero_exact_pct}} %) are exact and {{distinct_nonzero_in_band}} ({{distinct_nonzero_in_band_pct}} %) are in band. This is the number the abstract leads with.
4. **Designs larger than the device.** For {{over_device_compared}} of the compared designs ({{over_device_compared_pct}} %), Vitis allocates more than the {{device_capacity}} BRAM_18K of the part, so they could never be implemented on it and Vitis's numbers for them are never checked by an implementation run. Over the {{fits_compared}} compared designs that fit, {{fits_exact}} ({{fits_exact_pct}} %) are exact and {{fits_in_band}} ({{fits_in_band_pct}} %) in band, with Σ|err| = {{fits_abs_err}} against {{fits_vitis_bram_total}} BRAM_18K (weighted error {{fits_weighted_err_pct}} %). The low weighted error of the full view (next point) comes mostly from the oversized designs.
5. **Weighted error.** The weighted error is low because the largest designs (stream_hls and its WIP copies, hlsyn-vitis) dominate the summed Vitis BRAM, and those designs are mostly exact. The per-dataset table shows where the remaining error lies.

The harness subset is the first development set, before the full catalogue was available (from then on the whole catalogue served as the development set): polybench, polybench_pragmas, forgebench, machsuite, spector_hls and vitis_examples. On it, {{subset_exact}} of {{subset_compared}} designs are exact and {{subset_in_band}} are in band, with Σ|err| = {{subset_abs_err}}.

{{table:per_dataset}}

*Table 6.2. Accuracy per dataset (`tables/ch06-per_dataset.md`).*

The per-dataset view separates two kinds of source:
- **Benchmark suites written for HLS tools.** polybench, polybench_pragmas, spector_hls, soda, hp_fft_hls and vitis_examples are exact on every compared design. machsuite has {{ds_machsuite_exact}} of {{ds_machsuite_compared}} exact, and forgebench {{ds_forgebench_exact}} of {{ds_forgebench_compared}}.
- **Student course projects.** The three `class_ece8893_*` datasets hold the hardest cases. In `class_ece8893_spring_2025`, {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_exact}} of {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_compared}} designs are exact, {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_in_band}} are in band, and Σ|err| = {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_abs_err}}. These designs combine dataflow regions, hls::stream calls inside pipelined loops, struct and vector types and hand-placed pragmas, which is where most of the open issues lie (Section 6.7).

### Per-array agreement

A design total can be exact while two arrays err in opposite directions, so the totals above do not show that the decisions behind them are right. `compare.py` therefore also joins the two tools per array, by the C variable name (Section 3.4). Table 6.3 reports that join for the final run, together with how much of it can be scored at all.

{{table:per_array}}

*Table 6.3. Per-array agreement over the {{pa_designs}} compared designs (`tables/ch06-per_array.md`).*

- **Coverage.** Of the {{pa_rows}} array rows that carry BRAM on either side, only {{pa_aligned}} ({{pa_aligned_pct}} %) align by name. They hold {{pa_vitis_bram_aligned}} of the {{pa_vitis_bram_total}} Vitis BRAM_18K ({{pa_vitis_bram_aligned_pct}} %). A further {{pa_non_array}} ({{pa_non_array_pct}} %) are outside every memory table (m_axi adapters, FIFOs of further module instances) and cannot be matched per array at all.
- **Agreement where aligned.** {{pa_aligned_equal}} of the aligned rows ({{pa_aligned_equal_pct}} %) have equal BRAM_18K.
- **Exact totals.** Of the {{pa_exact_total}} designs whose total is exact, {{pa_exact_all_rows_zero}} also have every array row equal. In {{pa_exact_artefact_only}} more, every differing row is one that exists under its name on one side only ({{pa_exact_artefact_rows_hlsest_only}} rows only in hls-est, {{pa_exact_artefact_rows_vitis_only}} only in Vitis). Examples are the FIFOs of the generated stream_hls designs, which hls-est names after the MLIR value, and hls::vector streams. These are mostly naming artefacts, but the join cannot prove it. In {{pa_exact_real_mismatch}} exact designs, aligned rows differ ({{pa_exact_rows_both_differ}} rows) and cancel.

The claim this thesis can make per array is therefore limited. Where the names align, hls-est reproduces {{pa_aligned_equal_pct}} % of the rows, and {{pa_designs_all_aligned_equal}} designs are right in every BRAM-carrying row. The rest of the per-array agreement is not measured.

### A naive baseline

How much of the accuracy comes from the inferred decisions? `naive_baseline.py` prices the same MLIR without any of them (Table 6.4). B0 takes every local and global array at its declared size and prices it with the 7-series RAMB18 formula at two ports. It has no dead-array removal, partitioning, replication, FIFO, PIPO, instance or adapter rule. B1 adds only the LUT-RAM threshold (N5). Both are scored on exactly the {{all_compared}} designs hls-est is compared on.

{{table:baseline}}

*Table 6.4. Naive baselines against hls-est (`tables/ch06-baseline.md`; {{baseline_skipped_arrays}} arrays in {{baseline_skipped_designs}} designs have an element type the baseline's parser does not price and are skipped).*

B0 is exact on {{b0_exact}} designs, almost all of them designs without memories that need BRAM. Its error is {{b0_abs_err}} BRAM_18K ({{b0_weighted_err_pct}} % of the Vitis total), and {{b0_under}} designs under-count. The inferred rules, not the RAMB18 formula, carry the result.

## 6.3 Accuracy over the rounds

![Accuracy history](figures/ch06-history.png)

*Figure 6.1. Compared, exact and in-band designs, and Σ|err| (log scale), after each milestone (`figures/ch06-history.pdf`). The dotted line marks the switch from the exactclass shim to the real Vitis headers.*

{{table:history}}

*Table 6.5. Accuracy after each milestone (`tables/ch06-history.md`).*

The history starts at the first full HLSFactory_v2 run ({{history_first}}): {{hist0_exact}} of {{hist0_compared}} designs exact, Σ|err| = {{hist0_abs_err}}. The newest run ({{history_last}}) has {{histN_exact}} of {{histN_compared}} exact and Σ|err| = {{histN_abs_err}}. The curve has three phases:

1. **Structural channel rules (FIFO, PIPO).** The steep rise comes from hls::stream FIFOs (N31) and dataflow ping-pong buffers (N33). Before these rules, the model priced both kinds of channel as ordinary RAMs, or not at all. Each rule changed the estimate of many dataflow designs at once (Table 6.6).
2. **Memory-architecture rules.** Exactness then rises more slowly through RAM width narrowing (N35), process merges (N36), the inputs of the RAM_1WNR copy count (N38) and struct handling (N37). The last of these is the large Σ|err| drop at "structs (N37)".
3. **The switch to the real Vitis headers.** The number of compiled designs rises from {{histR0_cgeist_ok}} to {{histR1_cgeist_ok}}, and the number compared from {{histR0_compared}} to {{histR1_compared}}. Σ|err| rises from {{histR0_abs_err}} to {{histR1_abs_err}}. Per the CHANGELOG entry of the switch, the rise comes from the designs compared only under the real headers, and no exact design was lost. Rounds 6 to 10 then bring Σ|err| down again, each with a few proven rules.

The rise of Σ|err| at round 8 has a known cause, recorded in that round's CHANGELOG entry. A cgeist fix to cross-unit inlining (N68) exposed a separate banking problem on two Yolo-v3-tiny designs, and the window-read rule N74 of round 9 resolved it.

## 6.4 Error distribution

![Error distribution](figures/ch06-error_distribution.png)

*Figure 6.2. Left: the signed relative error of the designs that are not exact. Right: the cumulative distribution of |relative error| over all compared designs with a non-zero Vitis total. The dashed line is the {{band_over_pct}} % band (`figures/ch06-error_distribution.pdf`).*

![hls-est vs Vitis](figures/ch06-scatter.png)

*Figure 6.3. hls-est against Vitis per compared design, on log-log axes (BRAM + 1, so that zero totals can be drawn). The solid line is equality and the dashed line the {{band_over_pct}} % band (`figures/ch06-scatter.pdf`).*

The distribution is sharply peaked at zero. Of the designs that are not exact, most are within a few percent, and the over-counts cluster just above zero, inside the band. The out-of-band points fall into two groups:
- **Under-counts**, which make up most of them ({{all_under}} of {{all_out_of_band}}). The largest of them come from loops that Vitis pipelines but hls-est leaves sequential or schedules at a higher II, so a memory gets fewer copies (Section 6.7).
- **A few large relative over-counts on small designs.** For example, whetstone has 0 BRAM in Vitis, so any non-zero estimate is out of band.

## 6.5 What each rule contributes

Table 6.6 measures each modelled rule by a pair of full-catalogue runs. The pairs are of two kinds, and they must be read differently.
- **Controlled pairs ({{rules_controlled}} rows).** Since round 7 most rules have a configuration key. The "off" run sets that key to its earlier value on the same MLIR with the same binary, so the pair differs only in the rule. In round 7 a chain of runs switches the rules on one at a time.
- **Historical pairs ({{rules_historical}} rows).** Earlier rules are measured between consecutive published runs. Such a pair also contains every other change made between the two runs: other commits, rebuilt cgeist binaries, and in one row a switch of the front end. These rows are a history of the project, not a controlled measurement of one rule.

Only designs compared in both runs count. "changed" is the number of designs whose estimate moved; "band in / out" counts designs that entered or left the band. The "evidence" column gives the status of each N-note under the one status parser of this thesis (Section 3.3). "corpus only" marks a note confirmed on catalogue designs without a standalone kernel. Rows labelled O… implement an open issue without a separate Vitis note. Not every modelled rule rests on a minimal-kernel proof. The m_axi adapter rule cites N56, which superseded the corpus-only and unconfirmed N50. N1 was confirmed on the attention kernel, which is itself a catalogue design. The kernels were also chosen while chasing catalogue mismatches. The evidence column is therefore not independent of the catalogue it is scored on.

The row "real Vitis headers" is a front-end swap, not a Vitis rule; it is listed because it changed the compared set, and it is excluded from the ranking below.

{{table:rule_contributions}}

*Table 6.6. Per-rule contribution, ranked by the change of Σ|err| (`tables/ch06-rule_contributions.md`; the newly-exact design lists are in `data/ch06-numbers.json`).*

![Rule contributions](figures/ch06-rule_contributions.png)

*Figure 6.4. Designs newly exact and Σ|err| reduction per rule (`figures/ch06-rule_contributions.pdf`).*

The largest contributors are {{rule1_name}} ({{rule1_notes}}, {{rule1_ctl}}: Σ|err| {{rule1_derr}}, {{rule1_newly}} designs newly exact), {{rule2_name}} ({{rule2_notes}}, {{rule2_ctl}}: {{rule2_derr}}) and {{rule3_name}} ({{rule3_notes}}, {{rule3_ctl}}: {{rule3_derr}}). Most later rules are narrow. They fix one memory pattern that appears in a handful of designs. That is expected once the broad mechanisms are in place, and it is why progress per round slowed.

Most rules lose no design that was exact. In {{rules_lost_any}} rows a design stops being exact, {{rules_lost_total}} designs in all: {{rules_lost_list}}. Each case has a per-array explanation, as the adoption rule of Section 3.5 requires:
- The PIPO case is recorded in the CHANGELOG entry of the PIPO rule. The design had been exact only by cancellation: its channel array now equals Vitis, and a second array is still priced wider than the width Vitis narrows it to (O18).
- arati lab2 is recorded in the round 7 report: an array that now equals Vitis had cancelled an unmatched one (O54 (b)).
- hp_fft `original_C_style` is exact again one step later in the same chain, once its math core is modelled (N60).

Leaving the band is reported separately, because the "lost exact" column does not show it. In {{rules_left_band_n}} rows, {{rules_left_band_designs}} designs left the band; {{rules_left_band_back}} of them are in band again in the final run:

{{rules_left_band_list}}

In a row marked historical, the move out of the band can come from any change between the two runs, not only from the named rule. Every design still out of band appears in Table 6.9 with its current cause.

{{rules_zero_effect}} rules change no catalogue estimate: {{rules_zero_list}}. N78's latencies equal Vitis on its kernels, and no catalogue design's total depends on them alone. N65 is implemented: its code sinking makes `fl_pre2` equal Vitis. On `fl_pre` and on its only catalogue design (ben_hall lab3), the estimate still differs, because hls-est's II for that pipeline is 17 against Vitis' 2 (O87). The sinking alone therefore moves no catalogue total.

One rule row makes Σ|err| worse while being per-array correct: **N48** (each dataflow call site is its own instance). It is right per array: the conv processes' arrays are now counted once per call site, as in Vitis. But the new instances also multiply per-instance arrays that Vitis keeps out of BRAM (O67), an excess that the lower instance count used to hide. The front-end swap row also raises Σ|err|. It replaces a hand-written shim with the Vitis headers, trading a few totals for correct widths and for coverage (the decision recorded in MEMOS #66).

{{table:pass_ablation}}

*Table 6.7. Historical pass ablation of the round 5 pipeline, under the exactclass shim (`tables/ch06-pass_ablation.md`).*

The ablation is historical. It was run in round 5, with the exactclass shim and the pipeline of that time, and it has not been repeated on the final pipeline (Chapter 8 lists it as open work). Its compared counts vary between {{ablation_min_compared}} and {{ablation_max_compared}}, against {{ablation_ref_compared}} for the full pipeline, because removing a pass changes which designs finish within the time limit. The rows therefore do not share one denominator. The ablation removes one pass at a time from the default pipeline. Without the pass whose removal costs most, {{ablation_top_pass}}, {{ablation_top_lost}} designs stop being exact. Banking, the automatic pipeline / unroll / flatten decision (N3) and the loop scheduler carry the model. Without the decision Σ|err| is {{ablation_apu_ratio}}× that of the full pipeline, and without the scheduler {{ablation_sched_ratio}}×, because RAM_1WNR replication (N4) depends on the schedule's II. {{ablation_zero}} passes have no effect on their own. They are clean-up passes (canonicalisation, constant propagation, loop normalisation) that change no estimate on their own.

## 6.6 Run time

{{table:runtime}}

*Table 6.8. Run time per design (`tables/ch06-runtime.md`; the hls-est column is this run's own `elapsed_s` on {{time_rows_from_run}} of the {{time_rows_with_hlsest}} designs with an hls-est time).*

![Run time](figures/ch06-runtime.png)

*Figure 6.5. Left: the cumulative distributions of the hls-est and Vitis times per design. Right: the cumulative distribution of the speed-up (`figures/ch06-runtime.pdf`).*

{{time_n}} designs have both times. hls-est takes a median of {{time_hlsest_median_s}} s per design, and {{time_hlsest_under_1s}} designs finish in under 1 s. A Vitis run takes a median of {{time_vitis_median_s}} s. The median speed-up is {{time_speedup_median}}×; the 10th percentile is {{time_speedup_p10}}×. Over the whole corpus, Vitis needs {{time_vitis_total_h}} h, and hls-est is {{time_speedup_total}}× faster in total. hls-est is slower than Vitis on {{time_slower_than_vitis}} designs. {{time_hlsest_over_60s}} designs take more than 60 s, and the slowest stop at the 120 s limit. All of them are large designs whose dependence analysis or scheduling is still slow (O103, O105).

The comparison is not like for like:
- The hls-est time covers only the estimation. It does not include the cgeist front end, which `run_hlsest.py` does not time separately.
- The Vitis time is the wall time of the whole Vitis run on the Windows host. That includes start-up and C synthesis, but not RTL export.
- The harness runs eight hls-est jobs at once in WSL, while Vitis runs were scheduled differently.

The speed-up shows orders of magnitude. It is not a benchmark.

## 6.7 Designs still out of band

{{table:out_of_band}}

*Table 6.9. The {{all_out_of_band}} out-of-band designs of the {{newest_label}}, grouped by the cause class with the largest net error in the round-10 ranking (`tables/ch06-out_of_band.md`; every design in `tables/ch06-out_of_band_designs.md`).*

The classes come from the round-10 out-of-band ranking (OPEN_ISSUES O113). That ranking split each out-of-band design's error per array and assigned each part to a cause. "changed since ranking" counts designs whose total has moved since the ranked run. For those, the class records the dominant cause at ranking time.

The largest class is {{oob1_class}}: {{oob1_designs}} designs, Σ|d| {{oob1_sumd}}. These loops contain hls::stream reads or writes. hls-est leaves them unscheduled, because it has no timing for the stream calls, so it does not pipeline them; Vitis does (O54 (c)). The stream timing is proven (N82) and implemented, but it stays opt-in: switched on, it loses many designs that are exact today and so fails the adoption rule of Section 3.5 (O114). The reason it cannot be adopted yet is a separate gap, O1: loop-carried memory dependences do not reach hls-est's schedulers. The next classes are:
- {{oob2_class}}: the greedy modulo scheduler's II on large bodies (O56).
- {{oob3_class}}: an unroll factor on a pipelined loop with a run-time trip count.

Smaller classes are either proven rules that are not implemented yet, such as flattening across a stream access (N55), or rules whose Vitis trigger is itself unconfirmed: the do-while flatten refusal (N64) is unconfirmed as to which source shape causes it.

Out-of-band designs total Σ|d| = {{oob_sumd}}. {{oob_under}} of them are under-counts. The largest under-count classes are schedule-driven: an II that is too high, or a loop that is not pipelined, gives fewer RAM_1WNR copies (N4, N38). The over-counts are spread over several classes: RAM_1WNR copy counts, arrays Vitis keeps in registers or removes (O60) and an m_axi adapter whose INTERFACE pragma Vitis drops.

## 6.8 Threats to validity and what is not covered

- **BRAM only.** hls-est models BRAM_18K, the resource with the most discrete and most explainable allocation. DSP, FF and LUT (Phase 6) are not evaluated here.
- **In-sample evaluation.** The rules were found while working on the same catalogue they are scored on; there is no held-out test set. The harness subset was the development set only at the start; from the first full run on, the whole catalogue was. Many rules were found by chasing a catalogue mismatch and then proven on kernels written for that mismatch, so the kernels are not independent of the catalogue either. Three things mitigate this:
  - No rule may branch on design names, array names or specific sizes.
  - Most rules are proven on minimal kernels that are not catalogue designs ({{notes_confirmed_kernels}} notes are confirmed by kernels; {{notes_confirmed_corpus}} are confirmed on catalogue designs or ground-truth reports only: {{notes_confirmed_corpus_list}}).
  - Rules that fail the catalogue gate stay opt-in (for example N82).

  Even so, the exact-match rate partly measures how well the corpus has been studied. A fresh corpus, or a later HLSFactory release frozen against the final hls-est, would measure generalisation (Section 8.6).
- **Totals, not every array.** Exactness is judged on design totals. The per-array join (Table 6.3) aligns only {{pa_aligned_pct}} % of the BRAM-carrying array rows, and {{pa_non_array_pct}} % of the Vitis BRAM lies outside every memory table, so per-array correctness is measured only where names align.
- **Designs larger than the device.** {{over_device_compared}} compared designs ({{over_device_compared_pct}} %) need more than the {{device_capacity}} BRAM_18K of the part; the evaluation compares allocations, and for these designs Vitis's allocation is never checked by an implementation. The "fits the device" row of Table 6.1 reports accuracy without them.
- **Repeatability of the ground truth.** No Vitis run was repeated on purpose. The only evidence is indirect: {{rep_groups}} groups of designs ({{rep_designs}} designs) have byte-identical cgeist MLIR and the same top, yet were synthesised as separate Vitis runs with different cache keys. Their Vitis totals agree in {{rep_totals_agree}} of {{rep_groups}} groups and their memory tables in {{rep_memtables_agree}}. Identical MLIR does not prove identical C sources, and the Windows / WSL runner was the same for all of them, so a deliberate repeat of a sample of runs is still open.
- **Rule interactions.** Kernels sweep one parameter at a time. Of the {{inter_compared}} compared designs, {{inter_one_or_more}} change under at least one of the rule pairs of Table 6.6, {{inter_two_or_more}} under two or more and {{inter_three_or_more}} under three or more (at most {{inter_max_rules}}). Counting only controlled pairs, these are {{inter_ctl_one_or_more}} and {{inter_ctl_two_or_more}}. Historical pairs mix in other changes, so the first counts are upper bounds. Interactions between rules are therefore tested mainly by catalogue designs, not by kernels.
- **One target, one tool version, one mode.** The ground truth is Vitis 2025.1 on a single Zynq-7000 part (`xc7z020`) at 10 ns with `throughput_driven=off`. The `td=auto` behaviour differs (N2), for example in automatic array splitting, and is only partly modelled. The formula is the 7-series RAMB18 aspect-mode formula (N6, N8). For other families it is supplied as data by the configuration (`hls-doc/new-architecture.md`), but no other family has been measured against its tool. 
- **An academic corpus.** HLSFactory_v2 is mostly benchmark kernels and student projects. Some of it is duplicated (Section 6.1), and {{all_compared_vitis_zero}} compared designs have no BRAM at all. Industrial designs, with deep call hierarchies, large IP libraries and many interface variants, are not represented.
- **Ground-truth limits.** Some Vitis runs failed, timed out or were rejected (`done-exclusions.md`). Their designs are not compared, and the 120 s hls-est limit excludes a few large designs.
